ove and he will not  be able to solidify his position  by playing e6 the way that I've played  eats me so it's simply a very pleasant  position for whites so he sort of has to  take the challenge is said and take the  pawn I take de 492 and I remember I  spent a lot of time here on the previous  moves calculating calculating the forest  lines here not so much because I was  unsure of whether this concept was was  correct or not mainly I just found it so  fascinating to to look at these at these  forcing lines and I was just captivated  by them until I realized that I have to  move at some point and this time can be  spent calculating  later on as well anyway what he did I'm  just going to show a few further moves  he played f5 I went f3 he went if he  goes yeah three I take back with the  night you can see for instance if black  takes the night I take with the bishop  and this Bishop air is now extremely  powerful pointing against his coincide  the bottom b7 will fall the pawn on a  seven will fall and my bishops ruled the  board if he does not take on f3 I have  ideas like Knight to e5 with the same  sort of ideas opening up for for both  bishops and also Knight to g5 then later  on Bishop d5 check or knight c6 could  could wreak some havoc so he decided  quite cleverly to throw in the move e5  instead now he makes sure that in the  case  of d5 has happened EF 396 f3 that the  night is not coming to e5 and this is  also one of those cases where every  single one of my piece pawns are doubled  or isolated and yet it seems that every  time you have a bunch of isolated and  doubled pawns you have a lot of activity  and squares for the pieces right now for  instance the pawn on e3 is covering the  important d4 square making d4 a very  attractive post for for the night the  pawn on e5 it's not clear whether it's a  strength or weakness or I say we can see  in the game one more move rook to e8  threatening Bishop s 3 followed by  Bishop to e5 block would be very much ok 